PHZ — Collection Iteration ("each")

PHZ does not provide language constructs such as:

- "for"
- "while"
- "foreach"

Collection iteration is performed internally by the PHZ engine.

Scripts only describe what should happen.

---

The "each" Placeholder

"each" represents the current object while the PHZ engine iterates through a collection.

It is not a variable.

It is not stored inside the container.

It exists only while a PHZ execution property block is running.

Example:

.container:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .container2

    set = 1

}

Meaning:

For every object inside ".container:inside",

move that object into ".container2".

The iteration itself is performed by the PHZ engine.

---

Relative Collections

Inside a PHZ execution block,

:inside

always refers to the current container.

Example:

.room:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .storage

    set = 1

}

is equivalent to iterating over

.room:inside

---

Filtering Objects

"each" may be used inside expressions.

Example:

.room:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .parking

    set = EQ(:inside:each:type, "car")

}

Only objects whose "type" equals ""car"" are moved.

All other objects remain unchanged.

---

Modifying Attributes

"each" may also be used as the target of an assignment.

Example:

.room:{

    :inside:each:km =
        ADD(:inside:each:km,\1)

    set = EQ(:inside:each:type,"truck")

}

Only matching objects are modified.

Objects that do not satisfy the condition remain unchanged.

---

Multiple Operations

A single PHZ execution block may perform several operations for the same object.

Example:

.room:{

    :inside:each:processed = 1

    move = :inside:each

    to = .finished

    set = EQ(:inside:each:status,"ready")

}

For every object whose status is ""ready"",

1. set "processed = 1"
2. move the object into ".finished"

Both operations are performed during the same iteration.

---

Using "each" with LogTScript

PHZ execution blocks are executed only when their own "set" input evaluates to "1".

The "set" signal may be generated anywhere in LogTScript.

Example:

1wire moveReady = EQ(.motor:laps,\1)

.room:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .storage

    set = moveReady

}

The PHZ engine begins iterating only when "moveReady" becomes "1".

---

"each" and "on" Blocks

"each" is not available inside "on" blocks.

This is not valid:

on:1{

    show(:inside:each:id)

}

because "on" blocks contain only assignments.

They do not execute PHZ property blocks.

Instead, an "on" block should generate signals that later activate PHZ execution blocks.

Example:

on:1{

    moveReady = EQ(.motor:laps,\1)

}

Later,

.room:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .storage

    set = moveReady

}

When "moveReady" becomes "1", the PHZ engine executes the property block and creates the temporary "each" placeholder.

---

Design Notes

"each" is intentionally not part of the LogTScript language.

It is a temporary execution context created by the PHZ engine.

Because of this:

- "each" cannot be assigned to wires.
- "each" cannot be stored.
- "each" cannot be accessed outside a PHZ execution property block.
- "each" exists only while the corresponding PHZ property block is executing.
- "each" is recreated for every object processed during the iteration.

This design keeps PHZ fully declarative while remaining consistent with the LogTScript execution model.